---
/**
 * Security (MASTER_ROADMAP M20). The protocol's own policy is SECURITY.md in
 * the SAIPEN repository; this page restates it, links it, and adds the trust
 * model of this static website. No bounty, response-time promise or private
 * address is invented.
 */
import MaturityBadge from '../../components/MaturityBadge.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { SHORT_COMMIT, sourceUrl } from '../../content-engine/models/protocol';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Security"
  tag="/security/"
  description="SAIPEN security scope, supported versions and how to report a vulnerability, plus the trust model of this static website."
>
  <h1>Security</h1>
  <p><MaturityBadge level="stable" note={`restates SECURITY.md @ ${SHORT_COMMIT}; the repository file is authoritative`} /></p>

  <h2>Scope</h2>
  <p>
    SAIPEN includes a specification, a local Python runtime and install, uninstall and export tooling. Its documented
    security policy states that it runs no hosted service and collects no telemetry. The runtime manages project
    memory and supported host configuration. Operator-invoked Git and connector actions keep their own authorization
    boundaries; reading memory alone does not prove execution access.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your own instruction files</strong> (<code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>GEMINI.md</code>,
      <code>.aider.conf.yml</code>) are only changed by adding or removing a delimited <code>SAIPEN:BEGIN</code> /
      <code>END</code> block, and the original is copied to <code>&lt;file&gt;.bak</code> first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Skill directories</strong> created by the installer are SAIPEN-owned copies and are not backed up.
      Keep customisations in your own configuration block or a fork, not inside a copied skill folder.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is worth a report</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>A bootstrap script doing something to your filesystem or Git history beyond what its own comments and README describe.</li>
    <li>
      A real gap in the protocol's secrets-hygiene rule — never write keys, tokens or passwords into STATE, BOARD,
      LOG, KNOWLEDGE, kitchen, extensions, recovery or logs — that would make an agent following SAIPEN leak a secret
      into a committed file. Recovery and log sealing copy content verbatim by design, so anything that reaches the
      original is archived with it.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How to report</h2>
  <p>
    Open an issue in the <a href={SITE.issues} rel="noopener" data-external>SAIPEN repository</a>. For a real,
    currently exploitable problem, use the repository's <strong>Security</strong> tab ("Report a vulnerability") to
    file a private advisory instead, so it is not public before a fix ships. Include the script or rule, the concrete
    scenario, and what actually happens versus what should happen.
  </p>

  <h2>Supported versions</h2>
  <p>
    Only the latest tagged release on <code>main</code> is supported. SAIPEN is a protocol specification, not a
    long-lived service; there is no LTS branch. There is no bug bounty.
  </p>

  <h2>Checking what you install</h2>
  <p>
    SAIPEN installs from a Git clone, so the commit you check out is the integrity reference. For ecosystem binaries,
    the <a href="/downloads/">downloads catalogue</a> lists the SHA-256 digest GitHub reports for each release asset.
    This snapshot records digests; check each release page for current signature information.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Fully static HTML, CSS and fonts. No backend, no forms, no accounts, no uploads.</li>
    <li>No cookies, no analytics, no third-party requests — fonts and styles are served from this site.</li>
    <li>
      One value in your browser's local storage: the palette you picked in the theme menu. Nothing else is stored and
      nothing is sent anywhere.
    </li>
    <li>Three small inline scripts: the pre-paint theme restore, the theme menu wiring, and the Ctrl+K search shortcut. The playground and search pages add their own, run locally.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="w-panel__note">
    Canonical policy: <a href={sourceUrl('SECURITY.md')} rel="noopener" data-external>SECURITY.md</a>.
  </p>
</SiteLayout>
